<article class="env-lemma" id="0CIA">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0CIA">Lemma <span data-tag="0CIA">101.21.7</span>.</a> Let $f : \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Y}$ be a DM morphism of algebraic stacks. Then </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
For every DM algebraic stack $\mathcal{Z}$ and morphism $\mathcal{Z} \to \mathcal{Y}$ there exists a scheme and a surjective étale morphism $U \to \mathcal{X} \times _\mathcal {Y} \mathcal{Z}$. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
For every algebraic space $Z$ and morphism $Z \to \mathcal{Y}$ there exists a scheme and a surjective étale morphism $U \to \mathcal{X} \times _\mathcal {Y} Z$. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Proof of (1). As $f$ is DM we see that the base change $\mathcal{X} \times _\mathcal {Y} \mathcal{Z} \to \mathcal{Z}$ is DM by Lemma <a href="/tag/050F" data-tag="050F">101.4.4</a>. Since $\mathcal{Z}$ is DM this implies that $\mathcal{X} \times _\mathcal {Y} \mathcal{Z}$ is DM by Lemma <a href="/tag/050L" data-tag="050L">101.4.11</a>. Hence there exists a scheme $U$ and a surjective étale morphism $U \to \mathcal{X} \times _\mathcal {Y} \mathcal{Z}$, see Theorem <a href="/tag/06N3" data-tag="06N3">101.21.6</a>. Part (2) is a special case of (1) since an algebraic space (when viewed as an algebraic stack) is DM by Lemma <a href="/tag/050E" data-tag="050E">101.4.3</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>